BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[C1] SPECIAL EVENT: WIHP Orientation Session: Film & Sharing Circl
 e Debrief
DTSTART:20260901T190000Z
DTEND:20260901T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260903T235526Z
UID:f5278e21-4432-400f-b7c1-f3660445fb84_E198693@calendar.med.ualberta.ca
DESCRIPTION:\nCourse: MED 511\n\nInstructors:\n	Taylor Turvey (turvey)\n\n
 My Group:\n	Name: EDM\n	Location: 1080 Katz\n\nObjectives:\n	Reflect on th
 e history and legacy of residential schools and its impact on Indigenous h
 ealth from survivors' perspectives.\n	Reflect on the conditions under whic
 h residential schools were operated in Canada and the lasting impacts on s
 urvivors and family members.\n	Analyze the philosophical assumptions that 
 guided residential schools.\n	Discuss the impact of residential schools\, 
 disconnection from family\, culture\, community and land.\n	Reflect upon t
 he intergenerational trauma from the survivors perspective.\n	Describe the
  value of reconnection to culture\, language\, land and community to impro
 ve patient care.\n\nView this event online: https://calendar.med.ualberta.
 ca/events/198693
LOCATION:
URL:https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198693
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[C1] INTERACTIVE SESSIONS: Huddle Session 1:  Intro to PerMS Panel
  Discussion
DTSTART:20260918T160000Z
DTEND:20260918T175000Z
DTSTAMP:20260903T235526Z
UID:f5278e21-4432-400f-b7c1-f3660445fb84_E198695@calendar.med.ualberta.ca
DESCRIPTION:Welcome to the first Huddle session for Foundations\, which is
  a unique opportunity for students to see members of the Office of Advocac
 y and Wellbeing and select students speak to their personal experiences in
  medical education and Performance Mental Skills can help us navigate this
  challenging system. \nCourse: MED 511\n\nInstructors:\n	Cheryl Goldstein 
 (cgoldste)\n	Do Victor (vdo)\n\nMy Group:\n	Name: EDM\n	Location: 1080 Kat
 z\n\nObjectives:\n	Develop a personal PerMS action plan that identifies ke
 y skills and necessary resources for supporting ongoing well-being and aca
 demic success.\n\n	Describe how the consistent application of PerMS contri
 butes to their professional identity as resilient and compassionate physic
 ians \n\n	Apply self-regulation skills\, such as emotional regulation and 
 self-compassion to manage stress and maintain adaptive mental states durin
 g high-pressure medical scenarios.\n	Integrate all three components of Per
 MS (Mastery\, Mindset & Motivation\, and Self-regulation) to address compl
 ex medical challenges.\n\nView this event online: https://calendar.med.ual
 berta.ca/events/198695
LOCATION:
URL:https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198695
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[C1] LECTURE: WIHP Module 1:  Establishing a Framework for Indigen
 ous Health & an Alternate View of Canadian History (NR)
DTSTART:20260914T170000Z
DTEND:20260914T175000Z
DTSTAMP:20260903T235526Z
UID:f5278e21-4432-400f-b7c1-f3660445fb84_E198710@calendar.med.ualberta.ca
DESCRIPTION:Readings:\nde Leeuw\, S.\, & Greenwood\, M. (2011). Beyond Bor
 ders and Boundaries: Addressing Indigenous Health Inequities in Canada thr
 ough Theories of Social Determinants of Health and Intersectionality. In O
 . Hankivsky (Ed.)\, Health Inequities in Canada: Intersectional Frameworks
  and Practices (pp. 53–70). Vancouver\, BC: UBC Press.\n\nVowel\, C. Ind
 igenous writes: a guide to First Nations\, Métis & Inuit issues in Canada
 . (pages 30-39 pages 40-54\, pages 55-59).\n\nTruth and Reconciliation Com
 mission of Canada. (2015). Calls to Action. \n\nSee links below\nCourse: M
 ED 511\n\nInstructors:\n	Taylor Turvey (turvey)\n\nMy Group:\n	Name: EDM\n
 	Location: 1080 Katz\n\nObjectives:\n	Define the three main classification
 s for Indigenous people in Canada.\n	Define colonialism.\n	Explain the sig
 nificance of the land to Indigenous people in Canada.\n	Outline the purpos
 e and outcomes of the Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada with p
 articular focus on the 24th Call to Action.\n	Reflect on the university co
 ntext within which this course is practiced.\n	Describe two very different
  iterations of Canadian history.\n	Reflect on the social\, cultural\, and 
 political implications of storytelling.\n	Describe the Canadian government
 ’s response to the spread of infectious disease and starvation among Ind
 igenous peoples in Western Canada.\n	Explain the link between colonialism\
 , infectious disease\, and starvation.\n\nView this event online: https://
 calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198710
LOCATION:
URL:https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198710
END:VEVENT
