SUMMARY:[C1] LECTURE: Anti-Black Racism
DTSTART:20261109T210000Z
DTEND:20261109T215000Z
DTSTAMP:20260903T235526Z
UID:f5278e21-4432-400f-b7c1-f3660445fb84_E198798@calendar.med.ualberta.ca
DESCRIPTION:\nCourse: MED 511\n\nInstructors:\n	Eniola Salami (eniola)\n\n
 My Group:\n	Name: EDM\n	Location: 1080 Katz\n\nObjectives:\n	Discuss histo
 rical context regarding the historical Black/African Canadian racial exclu
 sion in medical education and health care service delivery.\n	Discuss how 
 to have critical conversations that acknowledge systemic Anti-Black racism
  in the healthcare system and provide a better understanding of race as a 
 social determinant of health.\n	Discuss common scenarios involving anti-bl
 ack racism in a clinical and academic setting.\n	Apply strategies to addre
 ss racism using scenarios/ practical examples.\n\nView this event online: 
 https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198798
LOCATION:
URL:https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198798
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[C1] LECTURE: Anti-Indigenous Racism and Cultural Safety
DTSTART:20261109T220000Z
DTEND:20261109T225000Z
DTSTAMP:20260903T235526Z
UID:f5278e21-4432-400f-b7c1-f3660445fb84_E198799@calendar.med.ualberta.ca
DESCRIPTION:\nCourse: MED 511\n\nInstructors:\n	Taylor Turvey (turvey)\n\n
 My Group:\n	Name: EDM\n	Location: 1080 Katz\n\nObjectives:\n	Describe the 
 determinants of Indigenous peoples’ health in relation to the impacts of
  colonial practices.\n	Recognize language\, culture and connection to land
  and family as protective factors and paths to wellness.\n	Define and disc
 uss the concept of “self-determination” as it relates to Indigenous pe
 oples\, communities\, and health.\n	Discuss the case studies of a First na
 tions person's experience accessing care.\n	Discuss the ways in which Anti
  Indigenous racism is embedded within the institution of health care.\n	Cr
 itically reflect on the role of health care professionals in addressing In
 digenous health disparities and health outcomes with attention to the TRC 
 calls to action under health.\n	Critically reflect on one's own understand
 ing of Anti Indigenous racism\, unconscious bias\, cultural safety\, cultu
 ral humility and Allyship.\n\nView this event online: https://calendar.med
 .ualberta.ca/events/198799
LOCATION:
URL:https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198799
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[C1] INTERACTIVE SESSIONS: Outbreak Investigation & Management
DTSTART:20261109T150000Z
DTEND:20261109T162000Z
DTSTAMP:20260903T235526Z
UID:f5278e21-4432-400f-b7c1-f3660445fb84_E198800@calendar.med.ualberta.ca
DESCRIPTION:\nCourse: MED 511\n\nInstructors:\n	Alexander Doroshenko (ador
 oshe)\n\nObjectives:\n	Define outbreaks of communicable diseases.\n	Descri
 be different types of outbreaks.\n	Discuss data types relevant to outbreak
  management.\n	Describe different types of studies used in outbreak manage
 ment.\n	Describe frameworks for public health management of outbreaks.\n	D
 escribe stages in outbreaks' investigations.\n	Outline the role of public 
 health professionals in management of outbreaks.\n	Apply analytical skills
  pertinent to outbreak management.\n\nView this event online: https://cale
 ndar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198800
LOCATION:
URL:https://calendar.med.ualberta.ca/events/198800
END:VEVENT
